---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp';
const title = 'Postscript vs Mailchimp for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Postscript vs Mailchimp for Shopify stores — SMS-native specialization vs legacy ESP with bolt-on text, stack architecture, and when to split channels.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Postscript or Mailchimp better for Shopify SMS?',
    answer: 'Compare the SMS jobs your Shopify store actually needs: documented opt-in, recipient eligibility, two-way reply handling, suppression and recovery ownership. Treat Postscript as an SMS candidate and Mailchimp as an email-led candidate whose current SMS regions, plans and workflow support require verification. A dedicated channel does not by itself prove better performance or compliance.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Mailchimp replace Postscript for a Shopify store?',
    answer: 'Replacement depends on the required workflows, supported destinations and operating costs, not a fixed SMS revenue percentage. Test campaign scheduling, replies, recovery cancellation and permission records with your intended plan. Keep any unsupported job with a clearly assigned sender until a replacement passes the same checks.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I keep Mailchimp for email and add Postscript for SMS?',
    answer: 'A split stack is a candidate architecture. Assign Mailchimp email jobs and Postscript SMS jobs only after checking current capabilities. Maintain separate purpose and channel permissions; an email subscription is not SMS permission. Recheck purchase, suppression and subscription state before dispatch, and document which system cancels each queued recovery message.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Postscript vs Mailchimp pricing compare for SMS?',
    answer: 'Obtain current written quotes for identical destinations, list sizes, message segments and annual peak volumes. Include email plans, SMS usage, carrier fees, registration, minimums, credits, overages, integrations and operator time. Compare a complete split stack with a complete consolidated stack rather than an SMS quote with an email subscription.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Mailchimp or Postscript?',
    answer: 'Sequenzy replaces Mailchimp for email lifecycle with pay-per-email economics and guided playbooks — pair with Postscript for SMS since Sequenzy does not replace SMS depth. Mailchimp still works if you want one login for basic email plus light SMS. Sequenzy plus Postscript is the modern split for teams graduating from Mailchimp without jumping to Klaviyo complexity.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which integrates better with Shopify?',
    answer: 'Check the installed Shopify integrations with a controlled event fixture rather than ranking integrations by reputation. Trace product variants, purchase events, audience updates and delayed or replayed events through each required job. Record latency and failure handling; confirm which sender owns cancellation and how stable event identifiers prevent duplicate recovery.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better deliverability and compliance for SMS?',
    answer: 'Neither vendor choice establishes legal compliance or delivery quality. Verify current destination coverage, registration, opt-out handling, quiet-hour behavior and reply routing with the relevant official documentation and your operating requirements. Imported email permission must not be treated as SMS permission; suppress an ineligible recipient before any promotional text is dispatched.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Propose a controlled test of keyword opt-in, recovery cancellation after purchase, reply routing and scheduled campaigns at quiet-hour boundaries. Include revoked permission, delayed events and duplicate replays. Record eligible sends and failures without predicting a winner. Separate attributed orders from incremental effect; a randomized holdout needs adequate sample size and uncertainty reporting before causal claims.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Postscript vs Mailchimp</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs Mailchimp for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          This comparison proposes Shopify workflow fixtures for an email-led stack and a dedicated SMS layer. The numeric merchant examples below are illustrative workloads, not observed tests or customer results. Current prices, regional availability and integration behavior remain unverified publication holds.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'SMS depth', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Compliance, two-way text, and Shopify cart recovery maturity.' },
            { label: 'Single-vendor simplicity', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'One login for basic email plus light SMS if volume stays low.' },
            { label: 'Flash sale SMS', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Segmentation, quiet hours, and conversational recovery.' },
            { label: 'Newsletter-first stores', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Familiar editor for brands not ready for ESP migration.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Graduate email from Mailchimp while Postscript owns SMS.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Postscript</th><th>Mailchimp</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>SMS-native Shopify marketing</td><td>Email-first with optional SMS</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>Usage-based plans</td><td>Free tier; paid from ~$13/mo</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Billing model</td><td>Plan + per-message</td><td>Contacts + SMS credits</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email depth</td><td>Not included — pair required</td><td>Core product (limited ecommerce)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS depth</td><td>Best-in-class for Shopify</td><td>Basic broadcasts and automations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify behavioral triggers</td><td>Core design</td><td>Adequate for simple use cases</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Two-way SMS</td><td>Core strength</td><td>Limited</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical role</td><td>SMS layer alongside ESP</td><td>Primary ESP with bolt-on SMS</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>What this comparison is actually about</h2>
        <p>
          Compare channel responsibilities first: email newsletters and lifecycle automation need an email sender, while SMS requires its own eligibility and recovery rules. Evaluate Postscript for the SMS role and Mailchimp for the email-led role, subject to verification of their current products. A split stack needs an explicit owner for every queue, suppression update and cancellation; familiar branding is not evidence that those jobs work.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Artisan Coffee Roaster: roast-date replenishment and wholesale sample follow-ups</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed numeric fixture, the artisan coffee roaster processes 860 orders/mo at roughly $52k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows roast-date replenishment and wholesale sample follow-ups, which means email must handle bean reorder at day thirty with subscription pause confirmations before holiday blend drop — not after support tickets spike. Evaluate Postscript for eligible SMS and Mailchimp for email workflows; verify the required integration and plan before assigning either job. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: suppress subscribers on pause from roast-date replenishment nudges.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose a peak-week priority schedule: keep necessary order-service messages separate from marketing, then coordinate eligible recovery, welcome education and promotional broadcasts. Purpose and channel permissions must remain distinct. Recheck current purchases, suppressions and subscription pauses at dispatch; choose one sender per recovery job and cancel stale queued messages in every participating system. Replay the same event identifier to test deduplication rather than assuming a tag sync prevents duplicate sends.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the coffee fixture, test a 300-unit holiday blend that sells out during a proposed two-hour campaign window. Check variant availability before content generation and landing-page routing; an email cannot promise inventory will remain available after dispatch. Use per-SKU roast dates, retail versus wholesale clocks and current subscription pauses. Treat forty-five-minute synchronization as a failure scenario to measure, not a safe universal suppression interval.
        </p>
        <h2>How climbing gear store emails through spring rack refresh</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed numeric fixture, the climbing gear store processes 1,100 orders/mo at roughly $89k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows seven-to-fourteen day harness research cycles, which means email must handle certification education before percentage-off nudges on ropes before spring rack refresh — not after support tickets spike. Evaluate Postscript for eligible SMS and Mailchimp for email workflows; verify the required integration and plan before assigning either job. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: branch browse sequences by fall-rating and harness fit pages.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose a peak-week priority schedule: keep necessary order-service messages separate from marketing, then coordinate eligible recovery, welcome education and promotional broadcasts. Purpose and channel permissions must remain distinct. Recheck current purchases, suppressions and subscription pauses at dispatch; choose one sender per recovery job and cancel stale queued messages in every participating system. Replay the same event identifier to test deduplication rather than assuming a tag sync prevents duplicate sends.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the climbing fixture, branch education by harness fit and intended use without inventing safety certifications. Test a shopper who browses on mobile, returns on another device and buys before a recovery message leaves the queue. Establish identity and channel eligibility, cancel obsolete reminders, and inspect delayed events. Compare education timing with a randomized holdout only if the sample supports a useful uncertainty interval.
        </p>
        <h2>Camping Equipment Merchant ($78k/mo) — route quiz outcomes into different welcome branches before tent promos</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed numeric fixture, the camping equipment merchant processes 1,350 orders/mo at roughly $78k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows tent-capacity quizzes and family car-camping segments, which means email must handle trip-season browse recovery on mobile PDPs before cart before Memorial Day camping spike — not after support tickets spike. Evaluate Postscript for eligible SMS and Mailchimp for email workflows; verify the required integration and plan before assigning either job. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: route quiz outcomes into different welcome branches before tent promos.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose a peak-week priority schedule: keep necessary order-service messages separate from marketing, then coordinate eligible recovery, welcome education and promotional broadcasts. Purpose and channel permissions must remain distinct. Recheck current purchases, suppressions and subscription pauses at dispatch; choose one sender per recovery job and cancel stale queued messages in every participating system. Replay the same event identifier to test deduplication rather than assuming a tag sync prevents duplicate sends.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the camping fixture, route tent-capacity quiz outcomes into family car-camping welcome branches before the Memorial Day promotion. A quiz submission is not automatically permission for every marketing channel. Test a mobile browse followed by a tent purchase and verify that the assigned recovery owner cancels the pending reminder. Log delays and replay behavior; do not infer channel superiority from attributed orders.
        </p>
        <h2>Before holiday starter-set rush: pottery studio retention choices</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed numeric fixture, the pottery studio processes 480 orders/mo at roughly $31k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows class waitlists and glaze-kit starter sets, which means email must handle kiln-care post-purchase and holiday gift exclusions before holiday starter-set rush — not after support tickets spike. Evaluate Postscript for eligible SMS and Mailchimp for email workflows; verify the required integration and plan before assigning either job. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: exclude class waitlist leads from glaze-kit promos after signup source tagging.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose a peak-week priority schedule: keep necessary order-service messages separate from marketing, then coordinate eligible recovery, welcome education and promotional broadcasts. Purpose and channel permissions must remain distinct. Recheck current purchases, suppressions and subscription pauses at dispatch; choose one sender per recovery job and cancel stale queued messages in every participating system. Replay the same event identifier to test deduplication rather than assuming a tag sync prevents duplicate sends.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the pottery fixture, keep class waitlist contacts separate from glaze-kit buyers and test signup-source tags against current purchase and subscription state. Schedule kiln-care education for the relevant product, then exclude gift buyers from unsuitable starter-set reminders. Assign queue ownership and test cancellation when a class booking arrives late; a stale tag must not authorize a promotional send.
        </p>
        <h2>Vinyl Record Shop: limited pressings with 200–400 unit runs</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed numeric fixture, the vinyl record shop processes 1,800 orders/mo at roughly $72k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows limited pressings with 200–400 unit runs, which means email must handle VIP drop allocation and grading education before promos before Record Store Day — not after support tickets spike. Evaluate Postscript for eligible SMS and Mailchimp for email workflows; verify the required integration and plan before assigning either job. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: suppress recent full-price buyers from instant discount blasts on scarce pressings.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose a peak-week priority schedule: keep necessary order-service messages separate from marketing, then coordinate eligible recovery, welcome education and promotional broadcasts. Purpose and channel permissions must remain distinct. Recheck current purchases, suppressions and subscription pauses at dispatch; choose one sender per recovery job and cancel stale queued messages in every participating system. Replay the same event identifier to test deduplication rather than assuming a tag sync prevents duplicate sends.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the vinyl fixture, use a proposed 300-unit pressing within the 200–400-unit range. Test VIP allocation, current variant availability and suppression of recent full-price buyers from immediate discount campaigns. Check landing-page availability after dispatch without claiming live email content can guarantee stock. Record duplicate-event handling and cancellation under a delayed purchase event.
        </p>
        <h2>How outdoor gear retailer emails through Memorial Day sale week</h2>
        <p>
          In this proposed numeric fixture, the outdoor gear retailer processes 2,400 orders/mo at roughly $112k/mo. Revenue rhythm follows layering guides and ultralight segment tags, which means email must handle Memorial Day tent promos only after education sends before Memorial Day sale week — not after support tickets spike. Evaluate Postscript for eligible SMS and Mailchimp for email workflows; verify the required integration and plan before assigning either job. The useful question is which contribution matches the job you will run weekly: merchandise ultralight versus family car-camping with different hero tents.
        </p>
        <p>
          Propose a peak-week priority schedule: keep necessary order-service messages separate from marketing, then coordinate eligible recovery, welcome education and promotional broadcasts. Purpose and channel permissions must remain distinct. Recheck current purchases, suppressions and subscription pauses at dispatch; choose one sender per recovery job and cancel stale queued messages in every participating system. Replay the same event identifier to test deduplication rather than assuming a tag sync prevents duplicate sends.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the outdoor fixture, compare layering education and ultralight versus family car-camping tent branches over a proposed ten-day consideration window. Record operator minutes spent changing audiences and hero products instead of claiming six hours saved. Distinguish campaign-attributed purchases from incremental lift; use randomized allocation, an explicit measurement window and uncertainty before drawing causal conclusions.
        </p>
        <h2>Mailchimp workflow reality: strengths, friction, and billing</h2>
        <p>
          Postscript shines when SMS is a primary revenue channel with compliance discipline. Its friction shows when email newsletters and lifecycle are the main stack. Mailchimp shines when broadcast muscle memory outweighs Shopify trigger depth. Its friction shows when cart and browse revenue attribution is load-bearing. Merchants that ignore friction points buy the wrong ceiling — or the wrong floor.
        </p>
        <p>
          Model a proposed year with thousands of waitlist contacts, flat order volume and a seasonal May campaign peak. Obtain matched quotes for the same contact eligibility, email sends, SMS destinations and billable segments. Include carrier and registration fees, minimums, credits, overages, integrations and operator time; do not claim that either billing model automatically rewards the chosen calendar.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Newsletter layer: editorial merchandising without discount training</h2>
        <p>
          Newsletters still matter. Coffee origin stories, vinyl staff picks, keyboard switch explainers, and climbing trip reports build trust before product blocks. Compare SMS product campaigns and email merchandising as separate jobs; the operational difference is how often you change hero SKUs mid-week and how live inventory syncs when a limited run sells out.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Pricing models that punish different mistakes</h2>
        <p>
          Use 12,000–18,000 contacts as a proposed quote range, not a claim of price convergence. Separate billable contacts from eligible recipients and retain suppression records when cleaning lists. Include any required email, SMS, capture and reporting tools in both annual totals; verify current terms rather than assuming optional modules or free tiers.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Hybrid stack patterns on Shopify</h2>
        <p>
          A candidate split is capture in a verified form tool, email lifecycle in Mailchimp and SMS in Postscript. Verify each current integration. Assign one recovery owner per channel and a shared priority policy; test cancellation, late events and replay deduplication across all queues before enabling overlapping flows.
        </p>
        
        <h2>Who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Postscript if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Sms is a primary revenue channel with compliance discipline</li>
          <li>You will use Shopify-native SMS with compliance guardrails weekly</li>
          <li>Message volume and plans fits your calendar</li>
          <li>Your team can maintain Postscript without external rescue</li>
          <li>Switching cost favors Postscript for the next twelve months</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Mailchimp if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Broadcast muscle memory outweighs shopify trigger depth</li>
          <li>You will use familiar editor and broad marketing features weekly</li>
          <li>Contacts by tier fits your calendar</li>
          <li>Your team can maintain Mailchimp without external rescue</li>
          <li>Incremental lifecycle GMV justifies Mailchimp migration</li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Choose the SMS layer and email layer by their demonstrated workflow fit. The proposed tests above have no observed winner. Run the five-job buyer test in both UIs, model twelve-month billing with real sends and list hygiene, and pick whichever platform lets you edit audiences safely the Thursday before a drop — not whichever sales deck had the prettier logos. See <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">selection framework</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">pricing models</a> before annual contracts.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-mailchimp">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-mailchimp">Sequenzy vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-brevo">Postscript vs Brevo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/vinyl">Email tools for vinyl record stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs Mailchimp FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
